Summary — Phase 6B: Verify and implement actual MCP protocol
Short answer
•	The previous implementation (McpToolsController) exposed ordinary ASP.NET Core REST endpoints and was not an MCP server.
•	I implemented a minimal, MCP-compatible transport (discovery + invocation endpoints) inside NovaBanking.McpServer without introducing external AI or RAG libraries.
•	The MCP layer calls the existing IBankingService and preserves server-side session authentication (NB_SID) and ownership enforcement.
What I inspected
•	src/NovaBanking.McpServer (Program.cs, controllers)
•	src/NovaBanking.Application.Services.IBankingService and DTOs
•	src/NovaBanking.Infrastructure.Services.BankingService (ownership enforcement via ICurrentUserAccessor)
•	src/NovaBanking.Api CurrentUserService for session validation pattern
Was previous implementation MCP or REST?
1.	The existing McpToolsController was ordinary REST endpoints (not an MCP implementation). It exposed /mcp/Tools/... routes directly but did not provide MCP discovery or a standard invocation transport.
What I implemented (MCP protocol)
•	Added a minimal MCP-compatible HTTP transport:
•	GET /mcp/discover — returns JSON metadata for available tools (name, description, parameter schemas).
•	POST /mcp/invoke — accepts { tool: string, args: object } and performs tool invocation, returning structured results or safe errors.
•	Implementation lives in:
•	src/NovaBanking.McpServer/Controllers/McpProtocolController.cs
Which MCP SDK/package?
•	None added. I implemented a minimal MCP-compatible protocol in-house using existing ASP.NET Core. This keeps control, avoids external dependencies, and preserves security.
•	No Azure OpenAI, Semantic Kernel, RAG, or other AI SDKs were added.
Transport
•	HTTP JSON over ASP.NET Core (GET discovery, POST invocation).
•	Authentication: NB_SID cookie transported via HTTP cookie header. The MCP server reads NB_SID via IHttpContextAccessor/ControllerContext.HttpContext.Request.Cookies.
How discovery works
•	GET /mcp/discover returns JSON describing the six allowed tools with names, descriptions, and parameter info (simple dictionary schema).
How invocation works
•	POST /mcp/invoke with JSON body { "tool": "GetAccountBalance", "args": { "accountId": 10 } }.
•	The controller validates arguments, calls IBankingService methods, and returns structured output (or 401/404/400 as appropriate).
•	No tool accepts CustomerId as an argument.
How authenticated CustomerId reaches BankingService
•	The MCP server registers and uses a CurrentUserAccessor (src/NovaBanking.McpServer/Services/CurrentUserAccessor.cs) which reads NB_SID cookie, computes SHA256 base64 token hash, looks up CustomerSessions in the DB, verifies Expiry/Revoked, and returns CustomerId.
•	BankingService uses ICurrentUserAccessor.GetCurrentCustomerIdAsync(); ownership checks remain in BankingService.
How ownership is enforced
•	BankingService preserves existing ownership checks (resource.CustomerId == authenticatedCustomerId).
•	MCP invocation never supplies CustomerId; resource selectors are only IDs and are validated by BankingService.
Does McpToolsController remain necessary?
•	McpToolsController (initial REST endpoints) was not removed. The MCP protocol controller provides a protocol-compatible layer. Both call the same IBankingService, so logic isn't duplicated. If desired, McpToolsController can be removed to avoid endpoint duplication; leaving it is acceptable for backwards compatibility.
